{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And his hands were faithful until the sun came.</b> For Amalek came upon them in the days of Moses, which was the time of their morning, the beginning of Israel's shining forth; and afterward, in the days of Mordecai and Esther, Haman of the seed of Amalek rose against them, and that was the time of Israel's evening sun, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וְלָעֶרֶב יְחַלֵּק שָׁלָל״ — this was in the days of Mordecai (Genesis 49:27). Therefore it says ״וַיְהִי יָדָיו אֱמוּנָה עַד בֹּא הַשָּׁמֶשׁ״, for the merit of Moses stood for Israel also in the days of Mordecai, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Megillah 13a): when the lot fell to Haman in the month of Adar he rejoiced with great joy, because in it Moses died — but he did not know that in Adar Moses was born; see in the Yalkut on Megillah. Hear from this that Haman supposed that Moses had died and his merit was finished, and he erred, for since he was born in Adar, this merit stood for Israel in that month. Therefore it says ״וַיְהִי יָדָיו אֱמוּנָה עַד בֹּא הַשָּׁמֶשׁ״. And therefore they established there these three pillars: Torah, as it says (Esther 9:27) ״קִיְּמוּ וְקִבְּלוּ״ — they upheld what they had already received; Avodah, through the fasts that were accounted like a sacrifice, and their outcry — that is avodah of the heart; and mishloach manot, that is gemilut chasadim." "Another matter: <b>Until the sun came.</b> Until the sun of Moses set, as it is written (Numbers 31:2) ״נְקֹם נִקְמַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מֵאֵת הַמִּדְיָנִים, אַחַר תֵּאָסֵף אֶל עַמֶּךָ״. And even though he heard that his death was dependent on this vengeance, none the less he did not delay to do the thing, for he desired in the house of Jacob to see their vengeance from their enemies in his lifetime. Therefore it says ״עַד בֹּא הַשָּׁמֶשׁ״ — all the while his hands were faithful to avenge the vengeance of Israel, to the point that he did not hold fast even against the setting of his sun, that is, his death, and so it is as though he surrendered his soul for them. And there is also an allusion to the days of the Messiah, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Deuteronomy Rabbah 3:16): ״YHWH בְּסוּפָה וּבִשְׂעָרָה דַּרְכּוֹ״ (Nahum 1:3) — ״בְּסוּפָה״, this is Moses, as it says ״וַתָּשֶׂם בַּסּוּף״; ״וּבִשְׂעָרָה״, this is Elijah, who ascended in the storm. And they learned from this that Moses too will come with Elijah when the Messiah comes, for it is said of him (Psalms 89:37) ״וְכִסְאוֹ כַשֶּׁמֶשׁ נֶגְדִּי״. Therefore it says ״וַיְהִי יָדָיו אֱמוּנָה עַד בֹּא הַשָּׁמֶשׁ״ — until the Messiah comes, ״וּכְאוֹר בֹּקֶר יִזְרַח שָׁמֶשׁ״."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:01.710398405Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}